<!-- AQL 2.5 EXPLAINED -->
<section style="padding:5rem 0; border-bottom:1px solid #1a1a1a;">
  <div class="container">
    <p style="font-size:0.65rem; letter-spacing:0.25em; text-transform:uppercase; color:#e00; margin-bottom:1rem;">Quality Inspection</p>
    <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.4rem,2.5vw,2rem); font-weight:200; color:#fff; margin-bottom:1.5rem; max-width:640px;">
      Understanding AQL 2.5 — what actually gets inspected
    </h2>
    <p style="font-size:0.88rem; color:rgba(255,255,255,0.7); line-height:1.85; max-width:720px; margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
      AQL (Acceptable Quality Limit) is the ISO 2859-1 sampling standard apparel manufacturers use instead of checking every single piece. An inspector pulls a random sample sized to your lot, counts defects by severity, and passes or fails the whole shipment based on two numbers: Ac (accept) and Re (reject).
    </p>
    <div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(220px,1fr)); gap:1px; background:#1a1a1a; margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
      <div style="background:#000; padding:1.75rem;">
        <p style="font-size:0.68rem; letter-spacing:0.1em; text-transform:uppercase; color:rgba(255,255,255,0.55); margin-bottom:0.5rem;">300-pc order (Code H)</p>
        <p style="font-size:1.4rem; font-weight:200; color:#e00;">50 pieces</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.8rem; color:rgba(255,255,255,0.6); margin-top:0.3rem;">sampled at random from the lot</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#000; padding:1.75rem;">
        <p style="font-size:0.68rem; letter-spacing:0.1em; text-transform:uppercase; color:rgba(255,255,255,0.55); margin-bottom:0.5rem;">Major defects, AQL 2.5</p>
        <p style="font-size:1.4rem; font-weight:200; color:#e00;">Ac 3 / Re 4</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.8rem; color:rgba(255,255,255,0.6); margin-top:0.3rem;">4+ major defects in the sample fails the lot</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#000; padding:1.75rem;">
        <p style="font-size:0.68rem; letter-spacing:0.1em; text-transform:uppercase; color:rgba(255,255,255,0.55); margin-bottom:0.5rem;">Minor defects, AQL 4.0</p>
        <p style="font-size:1.4rem; font-weight:200; color:#e00;">Ac 5 / Re 6</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.8rem; color:rgba(255,255,255,0.6); margin-top:0.3rem;">looser tolerance for cosmetic issues</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#000; padding:1.75rem;">
        <p style="font-size:0.68rem; letter-spacing:0.1em; text-transform:uppercase; color:rgba(255,255,255,0.55); margin-bottom:0.5rem;">Critical defects</p>
        <p style="font-size:1.4rem; font-weight:200; color:#e00;">Ac 0 / Re 1</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.8rem; color:rgba(255,255,255,0.6); margin-top:0.3rem;">a single safety defect fails the lot</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:rgba(255,255,255,0.6); line-height:1.8; max-width:720px;">
      Defects are graded by severity, not just counted: <strong style="color:rgba(255,255,255,0.8);">critical</strong> (safety issues — sharp objects, choking hazards), <strong style="color:rgba(255,255,255,0.8);">major</strong> (affects function or is clearly visible — broken stitching, wrong shade), and <strong style="color:rgba(255,255,255,0.8);">minor</strong> (small cosmetic issues that don't affect wear). Larger lots get proportionally larger samples but not a linear increase — use our <a href="/tools/aql-calculator" style="color:#fff; border-bottom:0.5px solid #444;">AQL calculator</a> for your own order quantity, or see the full <a href="/quality-inspection-service/" style="color:#fff; border-bottom:0.5px solid #444;">sample-size reference table</a> across lot sizes.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- FOB PRICE BREAKDOWN -->
<section style="padding:5rem 0; border-bottom:1px solid #1a1a1a;">
  <div class="container">
    <p style="font-size:0.65rem; letter-spacing:0.25em; text-transform:uppercase; color:#e00; margin-bottom:1rem;">Pricing</p>
    <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.4rem,2.5vw,2rem); font-weight:200; color:#fff; margin-bottom:1.5rem; max-width:640px;">
      What's actually inside an FOB quote
    </h2>
    <p style="font-size:0.88rem; color:rgba(255,255,255,0.7); line-height:1.85; max-width:720px; margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
      FOB (Free On Board) means the quoted price covers everything up to the goods being loaded on the vessel or aircraft at Chittagong or Dhaka — freight and import duty from there are on the buyer's side. As a general guide across apparel manufacturers, an FOB unit price is typically built from:
    </p>
    <ul style="list-style:none; padding:0; margin:0 0 1.5rem; max-width:640px;">
      {[
        ["Fabric & trims", "Usually the largest single component — yarn or fabric, plus zips, buttons, labels, and packaging materials."],
        ["Cut, make & trim (CMT)", "Labour to cut, sew, and finish the garment — varies with construction complexity and number of operations."],
        ["Wet processing", "Dyeing, washing, or printing, where applicable — priced separately from base CMT."],
        ["AQL inspection & compliance", "In-house quality checks and certification overhead (GOTS, BSCI, OEKO-TEX, etc.) baked into the unit cost."],
        ["Factory overhead & margin", "Utilities, compliance audits, and the factory's margin on the order."],
      ].map(([label, desc]) => (
        <li style="padding:0.9rem 0; border-bottom:1px solid #1a1a1a;">
          <p style="font-size:0.88rem; color:#fff; margin-bottom:0.3rem;">{label}</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.82rem; color:rgba(255,255,255,0.6); line-height:1.7;">{desc}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:rgba(255,255,255,0.6); line-height:1.8; max-width:720px;">
      What's <em>not</em> in FOB: ocean/air freight, marine insurance, and destination-country import duty — those are quoted separately and depend on your shipping method and destination market. Ask any apparel manufacturer for a line-item breakdown, not just a single unit price, so you can see where your money is actually going.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

